#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "3b27595a-5d6d-5ea3-92d2-6de7895c19fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-11153" :question/problem "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\left\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/403cdd2076f477fc63ccb7d639c01dd8.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v)$.\n\nDetermine the image of the right side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u = 2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u = 2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (u\\cos v, u\\sin v) \\\\\n&= (2\\cos v, 2\\sin v), \\frac{π}{4} \\le v \\le \\frac{2π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane.\nThe arc lies in the first and second quadrants between the polar rays\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/3fdee4f64327a57e730e6e81fa4f0aad.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b9d1a634-f1ef-5130-83ca-f3a555027699" :question/math-academy-id "e-12332" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/a62cb26f3c3f7dc30da2c43f45be00a6.png)\n\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v), \\, y(u,v))$,\n\nwhere\n\n$x = u \\cos v, \\qquad y= u \\sin v$,\n\nmaps the region $\\Delta$ from the $uv$-plane to $D$. Sketch the region $\\Delta$." :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3$, and\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$,\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/818d8bbe7fb53985dca3540435950c04.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4eaf3262-b6e3-57bc-9662-42ab52626ee9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12340" :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\bigg\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/30b7aa3de0ba4ae739a3cc65f315309c.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v)$.\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3$. Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (u\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}), u\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}))$.\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O$.\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3$. Substituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (u\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}), u\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}))$.\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O$.\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$. Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (\\cos v, \\sin v)$.\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$.\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$. Substituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (3\\cos v, 3\\sin v)$.\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between\n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3$, and\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$,\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/6e571d4127648d98fe51a7671f53c221.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "f4539253-b127-5d96-a331-3b357dc08a9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12363" :question/problem "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\left\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/ab40ef68e1926a48a712dca5721d947a.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v)$.\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1 \\leq u \\leq 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ninto our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (u\\cos v, u\\sin v) \\\\\n&= (u\\cos (\\frac{4π}{3}), u\\sin (\\frac{4π}{3})), 1 \\le u \\le 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwith the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/284be16ac10ed45614ba06b8ec36be32.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d7dbc3-7e7e-5c1f-83df-870e7589e46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193926} {:answer/id #uuid "c82bfff7-affe-5d8e-a085-68b9790436b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193927} {:answer/id #uuid "493b80cb-750e-5cd5-8b37-89649aaa3a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193928} {:answer/id #uuid "5024baa6-d1c7-556a-a434-59feab90505a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193929} {:answer/id #uuid "7bce1675-61a0-5a03-a0c9-a2c8da8d25eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bce1675-61a0-5a03-a0c9-a2c8da8d25eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193930} :answer-field/id #uuid "53e931a0-4ee8-5ca9-9d26-a653abca61cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193925}] :question/id #uuid "e90b093e-e3d7-5a59-aa98-97c5665b0473" :question/math-academy-id "q-160414" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{5} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8460c7ec-c603-520c-bebb-97ba5c9bfbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194064} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5ce761-e3e7-5055-bcd6-df184a8af782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194065} {:answer/id #uuid "df1894f4-aedc-51fb-acbe-cf8622d1c4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194066} {:answer/id #uuid "29808523-75ce-5128-bd03-aced76284423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194067} {:answer/id #uuid "394e9b9c-7f20-544a-abc1-9d22af0f884d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad5ce761-e3e7-5055-bcd6-df184a8af782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194065} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbb0286-69c6-5f89-b217-dc1d6f2d40f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194063}] :question/id #uuid "984dc00b-fd46-55ad-adb5-97d87c5df2f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161161" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T$, given by\n\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\n\nwhere\n\n$x = u\\cos v, y = u\\sin v$,\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D$. Which of the following could be the region $Δ$?"}]}
